int findBound(int[] nums, int target, boolean first) {
    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1, ans = -1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (nums[mid] == target) {
            ans = mid;                       // remember this hit
            if (first) right = mid - 1;      // keep looking left
            else       left  = mid + 1;      // keep looking right
        } else if (nums[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;
        else right = mid - 1;
    }
    return ans;
}

// nums = {1, 2, 2, 2, 5}, target = 2
// first=true:  match at 2 -> ans=2,right=1; match at... ends ans=1 (leftmost)
// first=false: match at 2 -> ans=2,left=3; match at 3 -> ans=3 (rightmost)
// count of 2 = last - first + 1 = 3 - 1 + 1 = 3
```

```java
// UNSAFE: left + right can exceed Integer.MAX_VALUE and wrap negative
int midUnsafe = (left + right) / 2;

// SAFE: right - left is bounded by the range size, never overflows
int midSafe = left + (right - left) / 2;

// Demonstration of the wrap:
int left = 2_000_000_000, right = 2_000_000_000;
System.out.println((left + right) / 2);          // -147483648 (overflowed!)
System.out.println(left + (right - left) / 2);   // 2000000000 (correct)
```
Both lines do the same arithmetic when values are small; only the safe form survives large indices.

